    <p>
      The two equations are <em>coupled</em>: <m>u_2</m> appears in the equation for
      <m>u_1</m> and vice versa, because the middle spring ties the carts together.
      Neither equation can be solved on its own. But the system is begging to be
      written with a matrix. Collecting the displacements into
      <m>\vec{u}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} u_1(t) \\ u_2(t) \end{bmatrix}</m>
      and differentiating entrywise,
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \vec{u}\,'' = -\frac{k}{m} A \vec{u},
          \qquad \text{where} \qquad
          A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      All the physics<mdash/>the masses, the springs, the geometry<mdash/>is now
      stored in the single matrix <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-oscillators-decoupling">
    <title>Diagonalization Decouples the Equations</title>

    <p>
      If <m>A</m> were diagonal, the two equations would not talk to each other at
      all, and each would be the familiar equation of a single mass on a single
      spring. So we diagonalize. The characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> is
      <me>
        \det(A - \lambda I) = (2-\lambda)^2 - 1 = (\lambda - 1)(\lambda - 3),
      </me>
      so the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = 3</m>, with
      corresponding eigenvectors
      <me>
        \vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      Following <xref ref="def-diagonalizable" text="type-global"/>, we assemble
      <me>
        P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        D = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        A = PDP^{-1}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now comes the decisive step: change coordinates so that the eigenvectors become
      the axes. Define <m>\vec{w}(t) = P^{-1}\vec{u}(t)</m>, so <m>\vec{w}</m> holds
      the coordinates of the displacement vector relative to the eigenvector basis.
      Substituting <m>\vec{u} = P\vec{w}</m> into the equation of motion and
      multiplying both sides by <m>P^{-1}</m>,
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \vec{w}\,'' = -\frac{k}{m} P^{-1} A P \, \vec{w} = -\frac{k}{m} D \vec{w},
        </mrow>
      </md>
      since <m>P^{-1}AP = D</m>. Written out, the new system is
      <md>
        <mrow> w_1'' \amp = -\frac{k}{m}\, w_1, </mrow>
        <mrow> w_2'' \amp = -\frac{3k}{m}\, w_2. </mrow>
      </md>
      The coupling is gone. Each equation involves only its own unknown, and each says
      that the unknown reproduces itself, negatively scaled, after two derivatives.
      By the calculus fact above, the solutions are cosines and sines:
      <md>
        <mrow> w_1(t) \amp = a_1 \cos \omega_1 t + b_1 \sin \omega_1 t,
          \qquad \omega_1 = \sqrt{\tfrac{k}{m}}, </mrow>
        <mrow> w_2(t) \amp = a_2 \cos \omega_2 t + b_2 \sin \omega_2 t,
          \qquad \omega_2 = \sqrt{\tfrac{3k}{m}} = \sqrt{3}\,\omega_1, </mrow>
      </md>
      for constants <m>a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2</m> determined by how the motion starts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Translating back with <m>\vec{u} = P\vec{w} = w_1 \vec{x}_1 + w_2 \vec{x}_2</m>,
      <em>every</em> motion of the two carts has the form
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \vec{u}(t) = \left( a_1 \cos \omega_1 t + b_1 \sin \omega_1 t \right)
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          + \left( a_2 \cos \omega_2 t + b_2 \sin \omega_2 t \right)
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This formula is the entire section in one line, and it is worth reading
      slowly alongside the animation in
      <xref ref="fig-coupled-oscillators-movie" text="type-global"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>The slow mode.</term> If the motion starts along the eigenvector
            <m>\vec{x}_1 = (1,1)</m>, it stays along it forever: both carts swing in
            unison at frequency <m>\omega_1</m>. Physically, moving both carts the
            same way leaves the middle spring at its natural length, so the coupling
            spring never acts<mdash/>each cart oscillates as if it felt only one outer
            spring, as <xref ref="fig-slow-mode" text="type-global"/> shows. That is
            the <q>mode 1</q> scene of the movie, where the middle spring stays quiet.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>The fast mode.</term> If the motion starts along
            <m>\vec{x}_2 = (1,-1)</m>, the carts swing in opposition at frequency
            <m>\omega_2 = \sqrt{3}\,\omega_1</m>. Now the middle spring is compressed
            and stretched by <em>twice</em> each cart's displacement, as
            <xref ref="fig-fast-mode" text="type-global"/> shows, so every cart
            feels a stronger restoring force and vibrates faster<mdash/>the larger
            eigenvalue <m>3</m> is precisely this extra stiffness. That is
            <q>mode 2,</q> where the middle spring works hardest.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Everything else.</term> A general motion is not simple<mdash/>but it
            is a sum of the two simple ones, in exactly the way a general vector in
            <m>\R^2</m> is a combination of the two eigenvectors.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-slow-mode">
      <caption>
        The slow mode, in two snapshots of the motion that starts along
        <m>\vec{x}_1 = (1,1)</m>. Both carts are always displaced by the same amount
        <m>u</m> in the same direction, so the distance between them<mdash/>and with it
        the length <m>\ell</m> of the middle spring<mdash/>never changes. The coupling
        spring is therefore never stretched or compressed and exerts no force at all:
        each cart swings as though held by its outer spring alone, which is why this
        mode is the slower one, at frequency <m>\omega_1 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>. Dashed
        lines mark the rest positions.
      </caption>
