      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      These special patterns of motion are called the <term>normal modes</term> of the
      system, and their frequencies <m>\omega_1, \omega_2</m> are its <term>natural
      frequencies</term>. The dictionary between the physics and the linear algebra is
      exact: normal modes are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, and each natural frequency is
      <m>\sqrt{k\lambda/m}</m> for the corresponding eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-oscillators-example">
    <title>Solving a Specific Motion</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-coupled-oscillators-release">
      <title>Pull One Cart and Let Go</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          At time <m>t = 0</m>, the first cart is pulled one unit aside while the
          second is held at rest in place, and both are released from rest: in
          symbols, <m>\vec{u}(0) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\vec{u}\,'(0) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Find <m>\vec{u}(t)</m>. This is exactly the <q>messy</q> motion of
          <xref ref="fig-coupled-oscillators-movie" text="type-global"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since both carts start at rest, every sine coefficient vanishes: the
          derivative of the general solution at <m>t = 0</m> is
          <m>b_1 \omega_1 \vec{x}_1 + b_2 \omega_2 \vec{x}_2</m>, and since
          <m>\vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2</m> are linearly independent this is <m>\vec{0}</m>
          only when <m>b_1 = b_2 = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cosine coefficients are the coordinates of the starting position in the
          eigenvector basis. We need
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = a_1 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            + a_2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          which gives <m>a_1 = a_2 = \frac{1}{2}</m>. Therefore
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \vec{u}(t) = \frac{1}{2} \cos( \omega_1 t)
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
              + \frac{1}{2} \cos( \omega_2 t )
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
          </md>
          or, cart by cart,
          <md>
            <mrow> u_1(t) \amp = \tfrac{1}{2}\cos(\omega_1 t) + \tfrac{1}{2}\cos(\omega_2 t), </mrow>
            <mrow> u_2(t) \amp = \tfrac{1}{2}\cos(\omega_1 t) - \tfrac{1}{2}\cos(\omega_2 t). </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The formula explains everything the movie shows. The second cart starts at
          rest, but its two cosines drift out of step and it begins to swing while the
          first cart calms down: the carts trade energy back and forth through the
          middle spring. And because <m>\omega_2/\omega_1 = \sqrt{3}</m> is
          irrational, the two cosines never return to step simultaneously<mdash/>the
          motion never exactly repeats itself, even though it is built from two
          perfectly periodic ingredients. The animation runs precisely this solution:
          equal parts of the slow mode and the fast mode, with frequencies in the
          ratio <m>\sqrt{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-oscillators-bigger-picture">
      <title>The Bigger Picture</title>
      <p>
        Nothing here depended on having exactly two carts. A chain of <m>n</m> carts,
        a vibrating molecule, a bridge deck, or a drumhead all lead to the same
        template: a coupled system <m>\vec{u}\,'' = -M\vec{u}</m> for some matrix
        <m>M</m> built from the stiffnesses, decoupled by diagonalizing <m>M</m>. The
        eigenvectors are the shapes in which the structure likes to vibrate, and the
        square roots of the eigenvalues set the tones. This is why engineers computing
        the resonant frequencies of a building and chemists reading the infrared
        spectrum of a molecule are both, at bottom, finding eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
